 f"Screening needs the right order more than the right number. Sorted into thirds by their own total effect, the causal transfer model placed {pct(DEC['causal_all']['tercile_agree'][0])} (95% CI {pct(DEC['causal_all']['tercile_agree'][1])}–{pct(DEC['causal_all']['tercile_agree'][2])}) of cities correctly, against {pct(DEC['full_all']['tercile_agree'][0])} for the full model and {pct(DEC['zone_baseline']['tercile_agree'][0])} for a baseline that assigns each city the median effect of its climate zone, which misplaced {pct(DEC['zone_baseline']['gross_misclass'])} of cities by two thirds (Table 1). Because the own effects share the identification assumptions of the causal model, we repeated the test against an independent benchmark: the warming observed at conversions dated by both land-cover products, net of pre-trends, in {XT['n']} city-regions. Against this benchmark the causal model again ranked cities best (Spearman correlation {B(XT['causal_all']['rho'])}), ahead of the full model ({m(XT['full_all']['rho'][0])}), the zone baseline ({m(XT['zone_baseline']['rho'][0])}) and the minimal model ({m(XT['minimal_all']['rho'][0])}); the cities' own estimates agreed with it at {m(XT['cs3_theta']['rho'][0])}. In European city-regions the causal model was closer in size (mean absolute difference {m(R4['eu_mae']['causal_all'])} against {m(R4['eu_mae']['full_all'])} °C), but the full model ordered them better ({m(R4['eu_rho']['full_all'])} against {m(R4['eu_rho']['causal_all'])}), so no model dominates everywhere. City-level 90% intervals for transferred effects (half-width {m(S['city_interval']['halfwidth'])} °C) remain as wide as the spread between cities; transferred effects order cities but do not replace a local estimate.",
